hdi08: bulk writeRX, and fix the batched ring push it needs

writeRX pushed one word at a time: a semaphore wait, a push and a semaphore
notify per word, three atomic read-modify-writes each. The Octatrack's eDMA
delivers every minor loop through it, so it was ~3% of the vCPU thread.
waitNotFull() is a no-op on a locking ring, so one batched push is
equivalent: the batched wait blocks exactly when the per-word waits would.

The batched emplace_back(count) it uses had never been called, and was
broken: the write count only advances after the loop, so every entry of the
batch was written to the same slot. Each entry now gets its own offset.

Measured unthrottled, interleaved: ahead in 4 of 5 rounds, ~+3 points of
real time. test-emu-audio 3/3 clean.

--- a/source/dsp56kBase/ringbuffer.h
+++ b/source/dsp56kBase/ringbuffer.h
@@ -91,8 +91,11 @@
 
 			m_writeSem.wait(static_cast<uint32_t>(_count));
 
+			// The write count only advances after the loop, so each entry needs
+			// its own offset: without "+ i" the whole batch lands in one slot.
+			const auto w = loadWriteRlx();
 			for (size_t i=0; i<_count; ++i)
-				_fillEntry(i, m_data[wrapCounter(loadWriteRlx())]);
+				_fillEntry(i, m_data[wrapCounter(w + i)]);
 
 			// usage need to be incremented AFTER data has been written, otherwise, reader thread would read incomplete data
 			incWriteCount(_count);
--- a/source/dsp56kEmu/hdi08.cpp
+++ b/source/dsp56kEmu/hdi08.cpp
@@ -152,13 +152,13 @@
 
 	void HDI08::writeRX(const TWord* _data, const size_t _count)
 	{
-		for (size_t i = 0; i < _count; ++i)
+		// One semaphore round trip for the whole batch instead of three atomics
+		// per word: waitNotFull() is a no-op on a locking ring, and the batched
+		// wait blocks exactly when the per-word waits would have.
+		m_dataRX.emplace_back(_count, [&](const size_t _i, TWord& _dst)
 		{
-			m_dataRX.waitNotFull();
-			const auto d = _data[i] & 0x00ffffff;
-//			LOG("Write RX: " << HEX(d));
-			m_dataRX.push_back(d);
-		}
+			_dst = _data[_i] & 0x00ffffff;
+		});
 		m_periph.setDelayCycles(0);
 	}
 
